"""Feature-first report controls; no tag queries or per-label / single-k gates."""


def explorer_markup():
    return '''<section class="card feature-workspace" aria-labelledby="ex-title">
<h2 id="ex-title">Features at a glance</h2>
<p>Choose an embedding, probe, neighborhood metric, and probe metrics. The selected settings apply to every feature row.</p>
<div class="ex-legend"><span class="ex-badge ex-good">Better relative to reference</span> <span class="ex-badge ex-bad">Worse relative to reference</span> <span class="ex-badge ex-up">More diverse / increased</span> <span class="ex-badge ex-down">Less diverse / decreased</span> <span class="ex-badge ex-warn">Limited evidence</span> <span class="ex-badge ex-neutral">Small change / descriptive</span></div>
<p id="ex-scope" class="muted" role="status"></p>
<details><summary>Choose a subset of features</summary><input id="ex-search" type="search" placeholder="Find features…" aria-label="Find features"><button id="ex-all-features" type="button">Select all</button><button id="ex-no-features" type="button">Clear</button><div id="ex-features" class="ex-features"></div></details>
<div id="ex-row-tools"><label for="ex-cohort">Rows to summarize </label><select id="ex-cohort"></select><button id="ex-reset" type="button">All rows</button>
<details><summary>Select a region in the embedding overview</summary><p>Drag a rectangle in the PCA view to select anchor rows. Neighbors still come from the full evaluated sample. PCA can distort distances; calculations use the original embedding graph.</p><canvas id="ex-map" width="850" height="300" aria-label="PCA overview; drag to select rows"></canvas><p id="ex-map-status" role="status"></p></details></div>
<div class="ex-controls"><label for="ex-embedding">Embedding </label><select id="ex-embedding"></select><label for="ex-probe">Probe </label><select id="ex-probe"></select><label for="ex-measure">Neighborhood metric </label><select id="ex-measure"><option value="diversity">Local entropy / numeric variance ratio</option><option value="agreement">Micro purity / numeric absolute difference</option><option value="lift">Micro lift / numeric Moran’s I</option><option value="macro">Macro purity / numeric local variance</option><option value="macro_lift">Macro lift / numeric absolute difference</option><option value="support">Known-neighbor share</option></select><label for="ex-mode">Show </label><select id="ex-mode"><option value="values">Measured values</option><option value="selection">Selected rows minus all rows</option><option value="models">Other embedding minus selected</option></select></div>
<fieldset class="ex-metric-toggles"><legend>Color-coded probe metrics</legend><div id="ex-probe-metrics" class="ex-metric-options"></div></fieldset>
<button id="ex-export" type="button">Export displayed measurements</button>
<p id="ex-readout" class="ex-readout" role="status"></p>
<div class="ex-table-wrap"><table class="ex-overview"><thead><tr><th>Feature / source summary</th><th id="ex-curve-heading">Neighborhoods across k</th><th>Selected probe metrics versus baseline</th><th>Brief interpretation</th></tr></thead><tbody id="ex-overview"></tbody></table></div>
<p class="muted">Probe colors compare the selected probe with the dummy baseline on the same held-out rows. Neighborhood colors use the selected measurement and configured descriptive thresholds; they are not significance tests. Faded k values are outside trusted_ks.</p>
<details class="ex-learning" open><summary>How to read intrinsic and extrinsic evidence</summary>
<h3>Diversity: what information varies here?</h3><p>Category entropy rises when labels are more evenly mixed; it falls when fewer labels dominate. The same full-run vocabulary normalizes every subset. Tags use the average binary presence/absence entropy across audited tags, in bits. This differs from the tag-occurrence entropy in source_summary.json. Unknown annotations are excluded. Numeric features use variance rather than arbitrary entropy bins.</p>
<h3>Neighborhood structure: is similar information nearby?</h3><p>Local entropy measures the mix inside neighborhoods. Low local entropy alongside diverse source labels suggests local organization, but low entropy alone can simply reflect a dominant label. Small neighborhoods also underestimate entropy. Increasing k changes the spatial scale and sample size, so an upward entropy curve is not proof of worse embeddings.</p><p>Purity rises when neighbors share the anchor’s label; for tags, only shared positive tags count. Micro weights positive anchor/tag pairs and macro weights labels equally. Lift divides each label’s purity by its full-run reference prevalence before averaging; a lift of 1 is reference-level agreement. A large lift for a rare label can be unstable. Missing neighbors change the effective k, which you can inspect using known-neighbor share.</p><p>For numeric fields, lower absolute differences mean nearby values are closer in the field’s own units. A local/global variance ratio below 1 means neighborhoods vary less than the full source; its denominator stays fixed on selection. Moran’s I rises with stronger neighboring covariation, but its scale depends on the graph. Absolute differences and local variance are not comparable across differently scaled fields.</p>
<h3>Probes: what can be recovered from the embedding?</h3><p>Each configured probe is a separate held-out prediction method. Classification and tag probes report metrics such as balanced accuracy, macro F1, accuracy, ROC AUC and average precision; numeric probes report R², MAE and Spearman correlation. Additional toggles expose prediction-versus-truth distribution diagnostics. The metric toggles select which values are shown and colored. Higher recovery scores are better; lower error and divergence are better; signed distribution differences improve toward zero. Distribution matching alone does not establish correct item predictions. Colors compare each selected metric with that feature’s dummy baseline; for MAE, color uses relative error improvement. Region selection rescoring uses only held-out rows and does not refit a regional model. These are single-split results, not universal feature rankings or encoder-training stability claims.</p>
<h3>Changes and confidence</h3><p>Selected-row changes compare with all evaluated rows, which includes the selection. Neighbors and prevalence references stay fixed; the selection changes anchor rows only. Model differences are B minus A on shared evaluable anchors. The detailed paired-comparison CSV reports conditional bootstrap intervals; overview color thresholds provide no uncertainty guarantee. Trust across k requires checking effective support, candidate-pool scope and supplied graph fidelity. No single k is automatically trustworthy.</p>
<h3>Scale to millions of rows</h3><p>Feature counts and moments can be streamed. Neighborhood summaries can be batched over a precomputed approximate-neighbor graph, with graph recall audited on a sample. Exact all-pairs search and a full row snapshot are not suitable for 8 million rows. This implementation is an in-memory sampled audit: row controls have explicit size limits, while the aggregate feature view remains available. Production-scale probes require sampled or incremental training plus a held-out evaluation stream; this report does not implement that backend.</p>
<p><a href="https://docs.scipy.org/doc/scipy/reference/generated/scipy.stats.entropy.html">Shannon entropy</a> · <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/model_evaluation.html">Prediction metrics</a> · <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html">Evaluation splits</a> · <a href="https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/scaling_strategies.html">Larger datasets</a></p>
</details></section>'''
